WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.251 --> 00:00:06.920
Let's get the obvious
out of the way first, shall we?

00:00:06.962 --> 00:00:08.880
Yes, I'm British.

00:00:08.922 --> 00:00:10.674
(Laughter)

00:00:10.715 --> 00:00:13.760
And yes, I'm here talking
to Americans about democracy

00:00:13.802 --> 00:00:20.016
on the eve of the 250th anniversary
of the Declaration of Independence

00:00:20.058 --> 00:00:21.393
from Britain.

00:00:21.393 --> 00:00:23.895
(Laughter)

00:00:24.521 --> 00:00:27.107
I'm very tempted to chuck
my talk out of the window

00:00:27.148 --> 00:00:29.484
and break into King George
from "Hamilton."

00:00:29.526 --> 00:00:30.777
"You'll be back."

00:00:30.777 --> 00:00:33.655
(Laughter)

00:00:33.655 --> 00:00:36.616
But I'm not here to call you back.

00:00:36.658 --> 00:00:39.119
I'm here to call all of us forward,

00:00:39.119 --> 00:00:41.871
because I've spent
the last ten years gathering stories

00:00:41.913 --> 00:00:44.791
from around the world
of the future of democracy

00:00:44.791 --> 00:00:46.001
for my book "Citizens"

00:00:46.042 --> 00:00:49.421
and for the podcast I co-host,
"How to Save Democracy."

00:00:49.963 --> 00:00:52.507
And what I've found
are a couple of things.

00:00:53.174 --> 00:00:55.677
The first is that there is a bold, bright,

00:00:55.677 --> 00:00:58.972
beautiful future of democracy
just wanting to take hold.

00:00:59.014 --> 00:01:03.226
It's emerging everywhere in institutions
that are open and participatory,

00:01:03.226 --> 00:01:05.353
and in politicians who are desperate

00:01:05.395 --> 00:01:08.982
to put the power of citizens
ahead of their own power.

00:01:08.982 --> 00:01:10.609
But what I want to focus on today

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:14.571
is not so much that destination,
as the journey to get there.

00:01:15.155 --> 00:01:17.240
Because what I've seen all over the world

00:01:17.282 --> 00:01:20.368
is that it's actually not institutions
that are changing for us.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:23.371
It's not politicians
who are leading us there.

00:01:23.413 --> 00:01:24.873
It's the people.

00:01:24.914 --> 00:01:28.877
It's citizens coming together
and building the future,

00:01:28.918 --> 00:01:32.380
creating the space
for the politics to move into.

00:01:32.964 --> 00:01:37.469
Because the line I want to leave you with
at the end of all of this,

00:01:37.469 --> 00:01:39.596
democracy is not something we have.

00:01:39.971 --> 00:01:41.973
It's something we have to build.

00:01:42.349 --> 00:01:44.893
Now, my favorite story
of this new democracy

00:01:44.934 --> 00:01:47.020
comes from Melbourne in Australia,

00:01:47.062 --> 00:01:49.397
and it starts at the end of 2020.

00:01:50.398 --> 00:01:52.317
In the context of COVID lockdowns

00:01:52.359 --> 00:01:56.029
and black summer bush fires
raging across Victoria

00:01:56.071 --> 00:01:58.114
and clogging the city with smoke,

00:01:58.156 --> 00:02:01.201
Melburnians were confined
to quarters for weeks on end.

00:02:01.951 --> 00:02:05.747
In that context, a few citizens
started to gather online.

00:02:06.373 --> 00:02:08.291
They were motivated by the understanding

00:02:08.333 --> 00:02:11.252
that the existing power structures
just weren't capable

00:02:11.294 --> 00:02:13.713
of responding to these challenges.

00:02:13.755 --> 00:02:16.633
And if they didn't want this to
be the pattern of the summers

00:02:16.633 --> 00:02:18.009
for the rest of their lives,

00:02:18.051 --> 00:02:20.929
they were going to have to lean in
and do something about it.

00:02:20.929 --> 00:02:23.682
Soon, those few citizens
were joined by a few more,

00:02:23.682 --> 00:02:27.936
and then tens and eventually hundreds
of people were attending these calls.

00:02:28.937 --> 00:02:32.232
They invited authors and experts
from around the world to inform them,

00:02:32.232 --> 00:02:33.775
as they tried to create a vision

00:02:33.775 --> 00:02:36.403
for the future of the city
that they wanted to hold.

00:02:37.195 --> 00:02:39.406
They got to this point
where they were saying,

00:02:39.406 --> 00:02:43.702
we believe we can create a resilient,
regenerative, beautiful city

00:02:43.743 --> 00:02:46.496
in a way that can be
a template for others to follow.

00:02:47.205 --> 00:02:50.542
They birthed an organization
called Regen Melbourne

00:02:50.542 --> 00:02:53.545
to act as a catalyst for citizen activity,

00:02:53.545 --> 00:02:57.382
and they aligned their work
around three earthshot goals.

00:02:57.382 --> 00:03:01.511
The first was to make the River Birrarung
swimmable for its entire course.

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:04.389
The second to localize
the city's food system

00:03:04.389 --> 00:03:08.268
so that every one of its neighborhoods
would be a nourished neighborhood.

00:03:08.309 --> 00:03:11.521
And the third was to foster
a sense of community

00:03:11.563 --> 00:03:14.649
on every one of the city's
300,000 streets,

00:03:14.691 --> 00:03:18.862
partly because if these situations
were to repeat themselves,

00:03:18.903 --> 00:03:20.613
then that's what would be needed,

00:03:20.613 --> 00:03:22.991
but more because -- joy.

00:03:24.826 --> 00:03:26.494
Five years on,

00:03:26.494 --> 00:03:30.123
there's been amazing progress
and in fascinating ways.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:34.294
Citizens are jumping into the Birrarung
ever closer to the sea

00:03:34.335 --> 00:03:36.045
at river festivals.

00:03:36.087 --> 00:03:39.299
The river has status as a living entity.

00:03:39.299 --> 00:03:40.842
And rights.

00:03:41.342 --> 00:03:44.220
But most interestingly
of all, on that goal,

00:03:44.262 --> 00:03:49.225
the City Council has come in behind
and adopted the goal with the citizens.

00:03:49.267 --> 00:03:52.270
And now institutional resources
are starting to flow.

00:03:52.312 --> 00:03:55.523
They've created the space
for the politics to move into.

00:03:55.899 --> 00:03:58.401
There's been similar progress
on the other goals,

00:03:58.443 --> 00:04:00.403
and now there are even conversations

00:04:00.445 --> 00:04:03.239
about even more ambitious projects
that they could take on.

00:04:03.281 --> 00:04:06.284
There's talk of creating
a standing citizens assembly

00:04:06.326 --> 00:04:08.369
as part of the city's
governance structure.

00:04:08.411 --> 00:04:11.831
Randomly selected citizens,
a representative sample of them,

00:04:11.831 --> 00:04:14.542
to debate and discuss
the key issues facing the city

00:04:14.584 --> 00:04:17.712
as a complement to the existing
elected systems.

00:04:18.713 --> 00:04:22.217
There are sister organizations
springing up all over Australia.

00:04:22.258 --> 00:04:24.427
The momentum is building hugely

00:04:24.427 --> 00:04:28.765
and it all started with a few citizens
coming together online,

00:04:28.765 --> 00:04:31.226
not staring into crisis

00:04:31.226 --> 00:04:32.936
but building out of it.

00:04:33.478 --> 00:04:36.231
Now look, there are so many stories
I want to tell you.

00:04:36.231 --> 00:04:37.732
I want another hour.

00:04:37.774 --> 00:04:39.234
(Laughter)

00:04:39.234 --> 00:04:42.946
I want to tell you about Taiwan's
crowdsourced COVID response

00:04:42.946 --> 00:04:45.490
that was rooted in a set of digital tools

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:48.493
that a citizen movement
came together and built.

00:04:49.118 --> 00:04:50.703
I want to tell you about Ireland,

00:04:50.703 --> 00:04:54.457
where there's a citizens assembly as part
of the national governance structure

00:04:54.457 --> 00:04:58.044
that has helped them resolve some
of the most polarizing issues of our time

00:04:58.086 --> 00:05:00.755
in a way that brought the nation together,

00:05:00.797 --> 00:05:03.466
and that the first iteration
of that assembly

00:05:03.466 --> 00:05:04.926
citizens built it.

00:05:06.928 --> 00:05:08.471
I want to tell you about things

00:05:08.471 --> 00:05:11.724
that are happening all over the world
and all over America as well,

00:05:11.766 --> 00:05:13.017
in Lexington, Kentucky,

00:05:13.059 --> 00:05:14.352
and Fort Collins, Colorado,

00:05:14.394 --> 00:05:16.062
and I don't have time.

00:05:17.689 --> 00:05:19.440
But what I can tell you

00:05:19.482 --> 00:05:21.860
is that there is something big going on.

00:05:21.901 --> 00:05:24.904
And I think the best way to understand it

00:05:24.946 --> 00:05:29.033
is through the lens of what I call
stories of self and society.

00:05:29.617 --> 00:05:31.619
And this is a framework
I want to offer you

00:05:31.661 --> 00:05:34.539
as a way of understanding
the moment in time we're living in.

00:05:34.539 --> 00:05:37.625
Back in the days of the old country,

00:05:37.667 --> 00:05:39.836
we lived in what I call the subject story,

00:05:39.878 --> 00:05:41.546
as in subjects of the king.

00:05:41.546 --> 00:05:43.715
The right thing to do
was keep your head down,

00:05:43.756 --> 00:05:45.758
do as you're told, get what you're given,

00:05:45.758 --> 00:05:46.968
on the basis

00:05:46.968 --> 00:05:48.595
that the God-given few knew best,

00:05:48.595 --> 00:05:51.764
and they would lead us to the best
outcomes for society as a whole.

00:05:51.764 --> 00:05:53.057
You'll be back, remember?

00:05:53.057 --> 00:05:54.225
(Laughter)

00:05:54.767 --> 00:05:58.897
Coming out of the two world wars,
America gave us the consumer story.

00:05:58.897 --> 00:06:01.399
The right thing to do now
was to pursue self-interest

00:06:01.399 --> 00:06:04.861
on the basis that self-interest
would add up to the collective interest,

00:06:04.903 --> 00:06:07.113
that that's how the best
society would result.

00:06:07.113 --> 00:06:09.324
With that came a kind of democracy,

00:06:09.324 --> 00:06:12.869
a democracy where we could express
our choice every few years,

00:06:12.869 --> 00:06:14.704
choose between a fixed set of options

00:06:14.704 --> 00:06:18.875
as to who to give our power to
on the basis of self-interest.

00:06:19.542 --> 00:06:20.752
And it seemed to work.

00:06:20.752 --> 00:06:23.046
It was a liberating shift
from what came before.

00:06:23.087 --> 00:06:26.633
But now we can't solve the challenges
of our time from within that logic,

00:06:26.633 --> 00:06:29.594
you can't solve loneliness
and mental ill-health

00:06:29.636 --> 00:06:31.763
from within a logic of self-interest.

00:06:31.804 --> 00:06:35.934
You can't solve pervasive inequality
from within a logic of self-interest.

00:06:35.934 --> 00:06:40.480
And we can't solve ecological crises
from within a logic of self-interest.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:43.399
As that story collapses,

00:06:43.441 --> 00:06:45.777
my way of understanding
what is going on right now

00:06:45.818 --> 00:06:48.363
is that the subjects story
is back in the uncertainty,

00:06:48.404 --> 00:06:52.617
with strong men leaders offering
false certainties into that chaos.

00:06:52.659 --> 00:06:54.494
But there is another option.

00:06:54.535 --> 00:06:57.789
To step forward into what I call
the citizen story.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:00.667
The logic here isn't keep
your head down, do as you're told,

00:07:00.667 --> 00:07:02.168
nor is it pursue self-interest.

00:07:02.168 --> 00:07:03.378
It's get involved.

00:07:03.419 --> 00:07:05.630
Contribute your ideas
and energy and resources.

00:07:05.672 --> 00:07:08.424
Come together with others and build it

00:07:08.466 --> 00:07:11.594
on the basis that all of us
are smarter than any of us.

00:07:11.594 --> 00:07:13.972
And that's how the best
society will result.

00:07:14.514 --> 00:07:17.475
And then there is Altadena, California.

00:07:17.517 --> 00:07:18.851
I get one more story.

00:07:18.893 --> 00:07:20.019
(Laughter)

00:07:20.645 --> 00:07:22.397
On January 25,

00:07:22.438 --> 00:07:26.067
the Eaton Fire tore through Altadena.

00:07:26.109 --> 00:07:27.819
Whole neighborhoods were destroyed.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:31.030
This historically significant
Black and brown community

00:07:31.072 --> 00:07:32.740
burned to the ground.

00:07:33.491 --> 00:07:34.993
In that context,

00:07:35.034 --> 00:07:39.080
what happened was that the citizens
of Altadena came together.

00:07:39.914 --> 00:07:41.332
Even though they had nothing,

00:07:41.374 --> 00:07:45.086
they came together to decide
what to do with what little they had.

00:07:45.086 --> 00:07:48.339
Neighbors helping neighbors,
giving and getting given.

00:07:48.339 --> 00:07:50.383
They created Dena Heals,

00:07:50.383 --> 00:07:53.386
a mutual-aid marketplace
and wellness center

00:07:53.386 --> 00:07:57.724
that has since helped over 7,000 people
from 1,500 families.

00:07:58.391 --> 00:08:01.144
And what I know from what
I've seen around the world,

00:08:01.185 --> 00:08:04.772
is that this is what happens
when crisis hits everywhere.

00:08:04.814 --> 00:08:07.942
Dena Heals is just
a particularly beautiful example

00:08:07.942 --> 00:08:10.611
of what is a deeply
human response to crisis.

00:08:11.195 --> 00:08:14.866
To let the barriers
that have kept us apart burn

00:08:14.866 --> 00:08:17.243
rather than our relationships.

00:08:17.952 --> 00:08:20.204
We come together in that moment.

00:08:20.204 --> 00:08:22.999
And Dena Heals isn't charity.

00:08:22.999 --> 00:08:24.834
It isn't even kindness.

00:08:24.876 --> 00:08:26.669
It's actually democracy.

00:08:27.086 --> 00:08:30.131
People coming together
to build what they need.

00:08:31.507 --> 00:08:33.843
This is what we will do
in times of crisis.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:37.722
But it would be so much easier
if we start building now.

00:08:39.265 --> 00:08:41.392
So here's what I want to leave you with.

00:08:41.434 --> 00:08:43.978
The first three words
of the US Constitution

00:08:43.978 --> 00:08:47.648
really tell the whole story,
written just a little way from here.

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:49.150
We the people.

00:08:49.692 --> 00:08:52.820
Because democracy isn't something
that is done for us

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:56.032
by institutions or by politicians,

00:08:56.032 --> 00:08:57.784
it's something we do,

00:08:57.825 --> 00:08:59.619
something we have to build.

00:09:00.119 --> 00:09:03.247
In this moment in time,
we all have agency.

00:09:04.123 --> 00:09:07.251
And it starts very simply,
on a Monday morning

00:09:07.293 --> 00:09:10.254
when you get back to your places
where you live and love,

00:09:10.254 --> 00:09:13.341
go for a walk, think
about what you care about.

00:09:13.341 --> 00:09:15.593
Think about who else cares about it too.

00:09:15.593 --> 00:09:19.514
Think about how you might gather
and what you might build.

00:09:19.555 --> 00:09:22.934
The future isn't going
to be made tidy for us.

00:09:22.975 --> 00:09:26.104
We're going to live through dark
and dangerous times.

00:09:26.604 --> 00:09:29.690
And it won't be delivered
for us as consumers.

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:32.652
But it doesn’t have to be done to us
as subjects, either,

00:09:33.319 --> 00:09:37.073
if we come together and build as citizens.

00:09:37.824 --> 00:09:39.033
So let's build.

00:09:39.367 --> 00:09:40.535
Thank you.

00:09:40.535 --> 00:09:44.497
(Applause)

